A Thermodynamically Admissible Constitutive Controller for Cyber-Physical Materials

Ouadi Maakoul

AI: Claude, Deepseek, chatGpt 

Abstract

Two adjacent research programs currently address parts of this problem separately. The first — thermodynamically-consistent neural constitutive modeling (TANNs, generalized-standard-material-structured networks, Meyer & Ekre's evolution-law discovery) — learns fixed constitutive laws offline from data, with thermodynamic admissibility guaranteed by construction, but the resulting law does not change once deployed. The second — “robotic materials” (McEvoy & Correll) — embeds sensing, actuation, and computation directly into composites, but without a thermodynamically grounded formalism governing what the embedded controller is allowed to do to the material's constitutive response. This paper proposes the missing object at their intersection: a Constitutive Controller $\mathcal{C} = (\psi, \Phi, \Theta, \Pi)$ — a free energy, a dissipation potential, an admissible control-parameter space, and an optimization policy — that lets an embedded cyber-physical system adapt a material's constitutive law online, in real deployment, while a proven invariant guarantees the Clausius–Duhem inequality is never violated. We establish a rigorous Thermodynamic Preservation Theorem by constructing $\Theta$ as a convex cone via a linear parameterization of the dissipation potential, making the projection operator well-posed and the proof independent of any quasi-static assumption on the controller. We further provide existence and uniqueness of the material response for any admissible control trajectory, a Lyapunov stability analysis of the projected-gradient controller, and a timescale-separation framework that bounds the error of surrogate objectives. The theorem’s scope is delimited explicitly: it covers dissipation-adaptive materials; the stiffness-adaptive case (where the free energy depends on the control parameter) is identified as a separate, harder open problem.

---

1. Introduction

The classical framework of continuum thermodynamics with internal state variables (ISV) provides the most rigorous basis for constitutive modeling of dissipative solids. In the generalized standard materials (GSM) formalism of Halphen and Nguyen [1], a material’s behavior is fully determined by two scalar potentials: a Helmholtz free energy $\psi(\varepsilon, x)$ and a dissipation potential $\Phi(\dot x; x, \varepsilon)$, where $\varepsilon$ is the strain, $x$ the internal state, and the convexity of $\Phi$ in $\dot x$ guarantees automatic satisfaction of the second law. This powerful structure has enabled a vast literature of physically admissible constitutive models.

In the last decade, the machine-learning community has revisited this framework to discover $\psi$ and $\Phi$ from data while retaining thermodynamic consistency. Thermodynamically-informed neural networks (TANNs) [2], GSM-structured networks [3], and automated evolution-law discovery [4] all produce constitutive laws that are fixed after training. While these learned laws are deployed with proven admissibility, their parameters never change post-training.

A parallel line of work in “robotic materials” [5] has demonstrated the feasibility of embedding sensing, actuation, and computation directly into composite materials, enabling adaptive structural behavior. However, these systems lack a thermodynamically grounded formalism: the embedded controller has no constraint that prevents it from driving the material into a physically inadmissible regime.

This paper introduces the Constitutive Controller $\mathcal{C}$, a formal object that bridges these two programs. The core idea is to parametrize the dissipation potential by a control vector $\theta$ that can be adjusted online by an optimization policy $\Pi$, while a Thermodynamic Preservation Theorem guarantees that the Clausius–Duhem inequality holds at every instant as long as $\theta(t)$ remains inside an a priori computed admissible set $\Theta$. This decouples the safety guarantee from the specific policy — the controller may employ any heuristic, consensus algorithm, or learned policy, provided its output is projected onto $\Theta$ before being applied to the material.

We present the mathematical foundation of this controller, correcting and extending previous informal proposals [6]. The key contributions are:

· A rigorous definition of $\Theta$ as a convex cone through a linear parameterization of the dissipation potential’s quadratic part, making projection well-posed and the admissible set easily checkable.
· A lemma and theorem proving that for a free energy independent of the control parameter, the pointwise dissipation inequality $A \cdot \dot x \ge 0$ holds along any trajectory with $\theta(t) \in \Theta$, without any quasi-static assumption on the controller’s speed. The quasi-static condition is relegated to the optimization policy’s objective meaningfulness, not to thermodynamic safety.
· Proof of existence and uniqueness of the material response for any measurable $\theta(t) \in \Theta$, using maximal monotone operator theory.
· A Lyapunov convergence proof for a projected-gradient-flow controller, connecting to the open problem of coupled mechanical/controller stability.
· A timescale-separation analysis yielding error bound shapes for surrogate objectives.

The paper explicitly delimits its scope: the current safety theorem holds only for dissipation-adaptive materials (free energy $\psi$ does not depend on $\theta$). The extension to stiffness-adaptive materials is left as a stated open problem requiring a generalized dissipation inequality that accounts for controller work.

