5. Well-Posedness of the Material Response

For any given control-parameter trajectory $\theta(t)$, the material response is governed by the differential-algebraic system

\sigma = \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\varepsilon}, \qquad A = -\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial x}, \qquad A \in \partial_{\dot x}\Phi(\dot x; x,\varepsilon,\theta).

The last inclusion can be inverted via the convex conjugate: $\dot x \in \partial \Phi^\star(A)$. Under the linear parameterization of §3.2, $\partial \Phi^\star$ is a single-valued Lipschitz operator.

Proposition 1 (Existence and uniqueness). Let $\psi \in C^2$ with $\partial_\varepsilon \psi$ strongly monotone, $\Phi$ as in §3.2 with $D_0 \succ 0$, and let $\varepsilon(t)$ be absolutely continuous. Then for any measurable $\theta(t) \in \Theta$, there exists a unique solution $x(t)$ on $[0,T]$ satisfying the evolution law for given initial $x(0)$. The solution is continuous and depends continuously on the initial data.

Proof sketch. The evolution law can be written as

\dot x = \big[\mathbf{D}(\theta(t))\big]^{-1} \big( - \partial_x \psi(\varepsilon(t), x) - \partial_{\dot x}\varphi(\dot x) \big)

(if $\varphi$ is differentiable) or more generally as a maximal monotone inclusion. Because $\mathbf{D}(\theta(t)) \succ 0$ uniformly (since $\theta(t) \in \Theta$ implies $\mathbf{D}(\theta) \succeq D_0 \succ 0$), the operator is strongly monotone and Lipschitz in $x$ (under appropriate regularity of $\psi$ and $\varphi$), guaranteeing existence and uniqueness by standard ODE theory or by the Brézis theory of evolution equations governed by maximal monotone operators. $\square$

This result confirms that the physical system is well-defined for any admissible controller, eliminating a potential source of mathematical inconsistency.

