6. Hierarchical Optimization and Timescale Separation

6.1 Four-Level Hierarchy

The controller’s optimization policy $\Pi$ is organized into four levels, matching the timescale separation typical of structural systems:

Level Object Timescale Example
0 Constitutive response ms $\sigma = \partial\psi/\partial\varepsilon$ at fixed $x,\theta$
1 Local adaptation seconds–hours cell-level $\theta_i$ update via surrogate $J_i$
2 Structural optimization hours–weeks consensus or global correction across the structure
3 Fleet optimization weeks–years cross-structure learning, closing the loop to offline retraining of the base $\Phi$ parameterization

Level 3 corresponds to the offline training phase of neural constitutive models [2–4]; Levels 0–2 represent the real-time online adaptation of the constitutive controller.

6.2 Timescale Justification and Surrogate Error Bounds

The optimization at Level 1 uses a local surrogate objective $J_i(\theta_i)$ that approximates the true structural-level cost. The accuracy of this approximation relies on a timescale separation $\tau_x \ll \tau_\theta$, where $\tau_x$ is the characteristic time of internal state relaxation, and $\tau_\theta$ is the interval between controller updates.

Suppose the fast mechanical subsystem reaches a quasi-steady state $x^\infty(\theta)$ when $\theta$ is frozen. Standard singular perturbation theory (Tikhonov’s theorem) guarantees that for sufficiently small $\tau_x/\tau_\theta$, the actual trajectory satisfies

\| x(t) - x^\infty(\theta(t)) \| = O\!\left(\frac{\tau_x}{\tau_\theta}\right)

under appropriate hyperbolicity assumptions on the fast dynamics. If the true objective $J$ is $L$-smooth in $x$, a Taylor expansion yields

J(x) = J(x^\infty(\theta)) + \nabla J(x^\infty(\theta)) \cdot (x - x^\infty(\theta)) + O(\|x-x^\infty(\theta)\|^2).

Hence the error incurred by using the surrogate $J_{\text{surr}} = J(x^\infty(\theta))$ instead of the true cost is of order $O(\tau_x/\tau_\theta)$. This provides a quantitative, albeit asymptotic, bound on the surrogate error (Open Problem 3 from the previous version). Concrete constants require specifying the potentials and the objective, which is deferred to the experimental validation.

