Appendix B. Proof of Lemma 1 (Pointwise Dissipation for Fixed $\theta$)

Lemma 1. Assume $\theta$ is held constant. Under the GSM structure with $\psi(\varepsilon, x)$ and $\Phi(\dot x; x, \varepsilon, \theta)$ satisfying the hypotheses of Appendix A, the instantaneous dissipation power satisfies
A \cdot \dot x \ge 0,


where $A = \partial \Phi / \partial \dot x$ (or $A \in \partial_{\dot x}\Phi$ in the subdifferential sense).

Proof. The GSM evolution law states that
A \in \partial_{\dot x} \Phi(\dot x; x, \varepsilon, \theta), \tag{B.1}


where $\partial_{\dot x}\Phi$ denotes the subdifferential of the convex function $\dot x \mapsto \Phi(\dot x; x,\varepsilon,\theta)$. The Fenchel–Young inequality for the pair $(\Phi, \Phi^\star)$ asserts that for all $v, A^*$,
\langle A^*, v \rangle \le \Phi(v) + \Phi^\star(A^*),


with equality if and only if $A^* \in \partial \Phi(v)$. Applying this to $v = \dot x$ and $A^* = A$, the inclusion (B.1) yields exact equality:
A \cdot \dot x = \Phi(\dot x) + \Phi^\star(A). \tag{B.2}


Now, since $\Phi(0) = 0$, the definition of the convex conjugate gives
\Phi^\star(A) = \sup_{w \in \mathbb{R}^n} \big[ A \cdot w - \Phi(w) \big] \ge A \cdot 0 - \Phi(0) = 0.


Moreover, by hypothesis $\Phi(v) \ge 0$ for all $v$. Hence both terms on the right‑hand side of (B.2) are non‑negative, proving $A \cdot \dot x \ge 0$. If $\Phi$ is differentiable in $\dot x$, then $\partial_{\dot x}\Phi = \{\nabla_{\dot x}\Phi\}$ and the argument is unchanged. $\square$

Note that the result holds pointwise in space and time for any fixed $\theta$; no assumptions about the rate of change of $\theta$ are required.

