Appendix E. Proof of Proposition 2 (Lyapunov Stability of Projected Gradient Flow)

Proposition 2. Consider the continuous-time projected gradient flow
\dot \theta = \mathrm{proj}_\Theta\big( \theta - \eta \nabla J(\theta) \big) - \theta, \tag{E.1}


where $\Theta \subset \mathbb{R}^m$ is closed convex, $J : \mathbb{R}^m \to \mathbb{R}$ is convex and $L$‑smooth (i.e., $\nabla J$ is $L$-Lipschitz), and $\eta \in (0, 2/L)$. Then for any minimizer $\theta^\star \in \arg\min_{\Theta} J$, the Lyapunov function $V(\theta) = \frac{1}{2}\|\theta - \theta^\star\|^2$ satisfies $\dot V \le 0$ along trajectories, and $\theta(t)$ converges to the set of Karush–Kuhn–Tucker (KKT) points of the constrained problem $\min_{\theta \in \Theta} J(\theta)$.

Proof. The projected gradient flow (E.1) can be written equivalently as the variational inequality
\langle \dot \theta + \eta \nabla J(\theta),\, \vartheta - \theta \rangle \ge 0 \quad \forall \vartheta \in \Theta, \qquad \theta \in \Theta. \tag{E.2}


Take $\vartheta = \theta^\star$, a minimizer. Then $\langle \nabla J(\theta^\star),\, \theta - \theta^\star \rangle \ge 0$ for all $\theta \in \Theta$ (first‑order optimality condition). Differentiating the Lyapunov function along the flow gives
\dot V = \langle \theta - \theta^\star, \dot \theta \rangle.


From (E.2) with $\vartheta = \theta^\star$ we have
\langle \dot \theta + \eta \nabla J(\theta),\, \theta^\star - \theta \rangle \ge 0 \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \langle \theta - \theta^\star, \dot \theta \rangle \le -\eta \langle \theta - \theta^\star, \nabla J(\theta) \rangle.


Using the convexity of $J$,
J(\theta^\star) \ge J(\theta) + \langle \nabla J(\theta),\, \theta^\star - \theta \rangle \quad \Longrightarrow \quad \langle \theta - \theta^\star, \nabla J(\theta) \rangle \ge J(\theta) - J(\theta^\star) \ge 0.


Thus,
\dot V \le -\eta \big( J(\theta) - J(\theta^\star) \big) \le 0.


This establishes that $V$ is non‑increasing, hence $\|\theta(t) - \theta^\star\|$ is bounded and the trajectory remains in a compact set. By LaSalle’s invariance principle for projected dynamical systems, $\theta(t)$ converges to the largest invariant set where $\dot V = 0$, which is exactly the set of KKT points (points satisfying $\langle \nabla J(\theta),\, \theta - \theta^\star \rangle = 0$ for some minimizer, i.e., stationary points of the constrained problem). $\square$

Remark. The same result holds for discrete projected gradient steps with appropriate step sizes, but the continuous-time version suffices for analyzing the timescale-separated limit where the controller evolves slowly compared to the material dynamics.

