Appendix F. Timescale Separation and Surrogate Error Bound

We formalize the two‑timescale structure and derive the asymptotic error bound for surrogate objectives.

F.1. Fast mechanical subsystem.
For a fixed control parameter $\theta$, the internal variable dynamics (see Appendix D) are
\dot x = F_\theta(x, \varepsilon(t)), \qquad \text{where } F_\theta(x, \varepsilon) = \mathcal{A}_\theta^{-1}\big( -\partial_x \psi(\varepsilon, x) \big). \tag{F.1}


Assume that for each constant $\theta$ and (quasi‑static) strain $\bar \varepsilon$, the system has a unique globally exponentially stable equilibrium $x^\infty(\theta, \bar\varepsilon)$, i.e., there exists a Lyapunov function with exponential decay rate $\lambda > 0$ independent of $\theta$.

F.2. Slow controller.
The controller updates $\theta$ at discrete times $t_k$ separated by intervals of length $\tau_\theta$, which is large compared to the relaxation time $\tau_x = 1/\lambda$ of the fast subsystem. Between updates, $\theta$ is constant and the mechanical subsystem relaxes toward $x^\infty(\theta, \varepsilon(t))$ (with $\varepsilon(t)$ varying slowly as well).

F.3. Tikhonov’s theorem and error bound.
Consider the singularly perturbed system
\begin{cases}
\tau_x \dot x = f(x, \theta, \varepsilon), \\
\dot \theta = \epsilon g(\theta, x),
\end{cases}


with $\epsilon = \tau_x / \tau_\theta \ll 1$. Classical Tikhonov theory ensures that, under the exponential stability of the boundary layer $x = x^\infty(\theta, \varepsilon)$, the actual trajectory satisfies
x(t) = x^\infty(\theta(t), \varepsilon(t)) + O(\epsilon)


uniformly on compact time intervals after a fast transient. In our setting, $\varepsilon(t)$ itself may vary, but as long as its characteristic time is no faster than $\tau_\theta$, the same estimate holds.

F.4. Surrogate error.
Let $J(x, \theta)$ be a smooth cost function (Level‑2 structural objective). The true objective accumulated over an interval $\Delta t$ is $\int J(x(t), \theta(t)) \, dt$. The surrogate used by the local controller is $\hat J(\theta) = J(x^\infty(\theta, \bar\varepsilon), \theta)$ for a representative strain $\bar\varepsilon$. The difference is
|J(x, \theta) - \hat J(\theta)| \le L_J \|x - x^\infty\| \le L_J C \epsilon,


where $L_J$ is a Lipschitz constant of $J$ in $x$. Hence the surrogate error scales as $O(\tau_x / \tau_\theta)$, justifying its use in the timescale‑separated optimization.

