Appendix H. Convexity Verification for the Linear Parameterization

We show that the dissipation potential defined in (A.1) is strictly convex in $\dot x$ for all $\theta \in \Theta$.

The Hessian of $\Phi$ with respect to $\dot x$ is
\nabla_{\dot x}^2 \Phi = \mathbf{D}(\theta) + \nabla_{\dot x}^2 \varphi(\dot x).


Since $\varphi$ is convex, $\nabla_{\dot x}^2 \varphi \succeq 0$. Moreover, $\mathbf{D}(\theta) = D_0 + \sum_i \theta_i D_i$ with $D_0 \succ 0$ and $D_i \succeq 0$. For $\theta_i \ge 0$, the sum of positive semidefinite matrices remains positive semidefinite, so $\mathbf{D}(\theta) \succeq D_0 \succ 0$. Consequently, the Hessian is positive definite uniformly in $\dot x$. Thus $\Phi(\cdot\,; x, \varepsilon, \theta)$ is strongly convex in $\dot x$ for every admissible $\theta$. This also implies that the subdifferential is a single‑valued, Lipschitz continuous gradient, simplifying all arguments.

