Appendix I. Remarks on the $\theta$-Dependent Free Energy Case

If Assumption A1 is relaxed to allow $\psi = \psi(\varepsilon, x, \theta)$, then differentiating gives
\dot\psi = \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\varepsilon}:\dot\varepsilon + \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial x}\cdot\dot x + \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\theta}\cdot\dot\theta = \sigma:\dot\varepsilon - A\cdot\dot x + \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\theta}\cdot\dot\theta.


The Clausius–Duhem inequality $\sigma:\dot\varepsilon - \dot\psi \ge 0$ becomes
A\cdot\dot x \ge \frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\theta}\cdot\dot\theta.


Thus, the controller’s change of stiffness is “paid for” by a power term that may be positive or negative. To maintain the inequality, one would need to either

· restrict $\dot\theta$ so that $\frac{\partial\psi}{\partial\theta}\cdot\dot\theta \le A\cdot\dot x$ (a dynamic constraint coupling the controller and the material), or
· extend the thermodynamic framework to include an external power supply from the cyber‑physical system, treating the controller as an open thermodynamic system.

A potential path is to define a generalized dissipation potential $\hat\Phi(\dot x, \dot\theta)$ such that the pair $(A, \partial\psi/\partial\theta)$ are its subgradients, thereby embedding the controller’s dynamics into an enlarged GSM structure. This remains an open research problem, as stated in the main text.

